(borge-bug (id "BORGE-1790535211")
  (title "json-values-alist verify misses the body boundary: tree/upsert + create/update kept VStr-only matching, so the documented tree/get + dict-set* + tree/upsert idiom raised bad-args (and notes/tree/save-outline, destructive-first, emptied a note before dying)")
  (status closed)
  (created 2026-09-27)
  (filed-by roerick)
  (doc "SPEC GAP (lisp-interpreter.json-values-alist). The subsection's
   verify stanzas lock the codecs (json round-trip) and dict-* behaviour
   \"diet-get/dict-set/dict-keys accept alist AND legacy VStr JSON input\")
   and the call-site migration it names (lookbook, filmstrip). It does
   NOT require the *consumers of those mutated values* to accept them:
   the data prims (tree/upsert, create, update) kept matching
   [VStr; VStr; VStr; VStr] exactly, so an alist body raised
   'need path kind type body-json'. That is the mutation idiom the
   notes chapter documents at borge/notes.borg (tree/get + dict-set* +
   tree/upsert at the same path) — the spec asserted an idiom its own
   implementation rejected. verify must assert the BODY BOUNDARY, not
   just the codec: a parsed-and-mutated node round-trips through
   tree/upsert/create/update.

   REAL-WORLD COST (2026-09-27, live prod). notes/tree/save-outline ran
   clear-headings (recursive delete of every heading) BEFORE writing the
   replacement and BEFORE any validation, so when write-root-body raised
   bad-args the note's headings were already deleted and never restored.
   One agent read-modify-upsert of the shared log/sessions doc left the
   note present but empty. Tree-time tombstones carry body=null, so the
   deleted heading content is not recoverable from tree_versions either.

   FIX (commit e1dc01c, this tree):
     - Prims_json.json_body: one body-boundary policy — VStr passes
       through, any structured value serializes via the shared emit
       codec; tree/upsert/create/update all use it (path/kind/type via
       coerce_str).
     - notes/tree/save-outline is now WRITE-THEN-SWEEP: upsert the new
       headings first, then clear-stale-headings removes only headings
       absent from the new outline (that helper already existed, unused).
       A mid-write failure now degrades, never empties.
     - outline/valid? is recursive (it only checked (count n) = 5 on the
       root, so a malformed CHILD passed and reached write-heading) and
       notes/doc/upsert-in refuses before any write.
     - 4 regression cases in lisp/test/prims_pure_test.ml pin the
       boundary policy.

   SPEC TO LAND ON THE NEXT PLAN PASS:
     - json-values-alist.verify gains: \"a structured (alist) body is
       accepted by tree/upsert / create / update; the legacy VStr body
       path is unchanged\"
     - the notes outline-save subsection states the ORDERING contract:
       an outline save must not delete content it cannot replace
       (write-then-sweep), and malformed outlines are refused before any
       destructive write
     - consider a durability rule for tree-time: a destructive op that
       cannot restore from its own history (tombstones carry no body)
       must be recoverable by the caller or guarded by validation.
FOLLOW-ON (verified on the dev image, same session): an eval that WRITES and
then raises does NOT roll back — (begin (notes/doc/upsert-in ...) (unbound-fn))
persists the write and still returns {\"ok\":false}. So the multi-step
save-outline sequence is non-atomic: each tree write autocommits. The
write-then-sweep order makes that degrade instead of destroy, but a partial
save is still possible (some headings written, sweep not run). Two options
for the next pass: (a) wrap notes/tree/save-outline in (with-transaction
(lambda () ...)) — with_dynamic_transaction joins an outer tx, so it is safe
under an existing one, and a budget/exception failure would roll back the
whole save instead of half-applying; (b) leave it and rely on write-then-sweep.
Worth deciding explicitly in the spec, since the notes chapter documents an
idiom (tree/get + dict-set* + tree/upsert) but says nothing about atomicity of
the outline save. NOTE: the kanban/move-card docstring claims \"/api/eval wraps
each request in one [transaction], and page handlers that need atomicity wrap
their own\" — the first half is NOT true as of the eio-floor refactor (verified
above); that docstring should be corrected too.


   RESOLUTION (2026-09-27, closed). Fixed on master e1dc01c, bug file
   committed 7e0a5be, deployed and VERIFIED live: prod serves release
   7e0a5be155b5 (boot_epoch 1790535780) and on that release, on the
   production habitat: (a) a structured (alist) body is accepted by
   tree/upsert — a scratch-doc append via tree/get + dict-set* +
   tree/upsert succeeded; (b) outline/valid? returns false for a
   malformed CHILD node; (c) notes/tree/save-outline's bound body is
   write-root-body -> write-heading fold -> clear-stale-headings (the
   write-then-sweep order); (d) an end-to-end scratch note went create ->
   two headings -> sweep to one -> malformed upsert refused with the
   surviving heading intact. Tests: four new json_body cases in
   lisp/test/prims_pure_test.ml (71 green in prims_pure); dune runtest
   lisp common green. agent/test rail-trees fails identically on HEAD
   (pre-existing, recorded in the json-values-alist agent note).

   STILL OPEN (do not lose these in the close):
     - the three spec-text items above (verify body boundary; the notes
       outline-save ORDERING contract; a tree-time durability rule) are
       NOT yet written into the .borg book — this bug tracks the gap and
       closing it does not land them. Carry them into the next plan pass.
     - save-outline is still NON-ATOMIC: a raising eval does not roll
       back (verified live on the dev image), so a mid-sequence failure
       can half-apply. write-then-sweep makes that degrade rather than
       destroy. Wrap in (with-transaction ...) if full atomicity is
       wanted.
     - kanban/move-card's docstring claim that \"/api/eval wraps each
       request in one [transaction]\" is stale (same live check).
     - tree-time tombstones carry body=null: deleted heading content is
       not recoverable from tree history; destructive ops that cannot
       self-restore need validation (now present) or a before-image.

   FOLLOW-ON CLOSED (b4337b5, 2026-09-27). The atomicity item is DONE, and
   it turned out to be a code bug against a spec that ALREADY required the
   transaction: notes.pipeline-model MUTATIONS ARE TREE ACTIONS says \"wrap
   the N affected cards in with-transaction so the reorder is atomic\" and
   \"RENAME A COLUMN: ... in one with-transaction ... all-or-nothing\", while
   libs/kanban/lib.pp had ZERO with-transaction calls. Wrapped:
   notes/tree/save-outline, kanban/move-card, kanban/swap,
   kanban/rename-column. Safe because nested with-transaction JOINS
   (store_db.with_dynamic_transaction rides an active tx) and it does roll
   back a raise (both verified live). Verified end-to-end on dev AND prod:
   a malformed outline now leaves the note COMPLETELY unchanged where it
   previously swept every heading, and valid saves still write + sweep.

   STILL OPEN (carry into the next plan pass):
     - notes.storage SAVE ORDERING CONTRACT now states write-then-sweep +
       validate-before-destroying + the atomicity wrap; the tree-time
       DURABILITY RULE and the json-values-alist BODY BOUNDARY are landed.
       What remains is the notes/doc/upsert-in path ITSELF: it writes the
       note row and then calls save-outline; each is now internally atomic
       but the PAIR is not (a failure between them leaves a new note row
       with the old outline). Wrapping upsert-in as a whole is the same
       one-line treatment if wanted.
     - tree-time tombstones still carry body=null (unchanged design); the
       rule now says not to rely on history for delete recovery.")
)
